# Maintain {{ project_name }}
{%- set published = kind == 'library' and publish_crate %}

This page is for the repository's owner. It lists the work that needs the owner's rights, in the
order it comes up: setting up the repository, releasing, changing an answer, updating trellis and
renewing the release token.

## Before the first push

The release workflow runs on every push to `main`, so set the repository up before you push.
Commit the generated project on a branch named `main` (`git init --initial-branch main`), then:

1. Create the repository on GitHub as {{ visibility }}, as you answered, without pushing:

   ```sh
   gh repo create {{ github_owner }}/{{ project_name }} --{{ visibility }} --source .
   ```
{%- if visibility == 'private' %}

   A private repository's Actions minutes come out of the account's included usage.
{%- if kind != 'service' %}
   A macOS minute costs about ten times a Linux one, and a Windows minute about twice.
{%- endif %}
   [Change an answer](#change-an-answer) says how to make it public.
{%- elif kind != 'library' %}

   Without GitHub Enterprise Cloud, GitHub attests release files only in public repositories, and
   the release workflow stops when it can't attest. [Change an answer](#change-an-answer) says how
   to make the repository private.
{%- endif %}

2. Set the repository up. The recipe needs `gh` signed in as an admin of the repository, and the
   just version the project pins, since an older just can't read the `justfile`. On Windows, run
   it in Git Bash:

   ```sh
   cargo binstall just@1.59.0
   just setup-github
   ```

   It allows squash merging only, with the pull request's title and description as the commit, so
   the title becomes the commit on `main`. It creates the `release` label that release-plz puts on
   its pull request.
{%- if visibility == 'public' %}
   It adds the Actions policy that allows `pull_request_target` for `rule-changes.yml`; without
   it, a public repository doesn't run the rule-change check, and no pull request gets the "Rule
   changes stand alone" status. It turns on private vulnerability reporting, which
   [SECURITY.md](../SECURITY.md) points to.
{%- endif %}

   Then it protects `main` with a ruleset: no deleting or force-pushing `main`, no change without
   a pull request, and three required checks from GitHub Actions: `PR title`, `CI passed` and the
   "Rule changes stand alone" status. A ruleset can require checks that haven't run yet, so `main`
   is protected from the first push. `CI passed` passes only when every other CI job passes, so
   the required checks stay the same when CI gains or loses a job.
{%- if kind == 'binary' %}
   The `Release build` checks come from another workflow and run only when a pull request changes
   what goes into a release, so they aren't required.
{%- elif kind == 'service' %}
   `CI passed` also needs `Container image`, which builds the image, as `just image` does, and runs
   it with `--version`, so the service must answer `--version` and exit 0.
{%- endif %}
{%- if visibility == 'private' %}

   Protecting `main` in a private repository needs GitHub Pro or a paid organization plan. Without
   one, the recipe says so and sets up the rest, and nothing stops a pull request whose checks
   fail, or one that changes rules and code together, from merging.
{%- endif %}

   Running it again sets the same values and creates nothing twice. `just setup-github --dry-run`
   prints each `gh` call instead of running it.

3. Install the [Renovate app](https://github.com/apps/renovate) on the repository. Each week it
   opens one pull request with the crate updates other than major ones, and one for each major
   update. The toolchain, tool, action and image pins arrive with trellis updates.

4. Create a fine-grained token with contents and pull request write access to this repository,
   and add it as the `RELEASE_PLZ_TOKEN` secret with `gh secret set RELEASE_PLZ_TOKEN`. A pull
   request that `GITHUB_TOKEN` opens starts no `pull_request_target` workflow, so without the
   token the release pull request never gets the "Rule changes stand alone" status and can't
   merge.

5. Check the settings. `just check-github` reports each one as ok or wrong, and fails if any is
   wrong. It checks that the `RELEASE_PLZ_TOKEN` secret exists, but never reads its value.
{%- if visibility == 'private' %}
   With no ruleset, as on a plan that can't protect `main`, it reports `main`'s protection as
   unavailable rather than wrong.
{%- endif %}
   On Windows, run it in Git Bash too:

   ```sh
   just check-github
   ```

6. Push `main`:

   ```sh
   git push --set-upstream origin main
   ```
{%- if published %}

## At the first release

Publish the first version by hand with `cargo publish`. If that commit has no `vX.Y.Z` tag, tag it
with its version and push the tag, so release-plz starts from it. Then add this repository's
`release.yml` as a trusted publisher in the crate's settings on crates.io. Later releases publish
through it.
{%- elif kind == 'service' and visibility == 'public' %}

## At the first release

Once the first release has pushed the image, make the `{{ project_name }}` package public in its
package settings on GitHub, so others can pull the image. GitHub can't make it private again.
{%- endif %}

## Each release

release-plz keeps a release pull request open with the next version and its `CHANGELOG.md` entry.
Merging it releases that version, with that entry as the release notes;
[release.yml](../.github/workflows/release.yml) does the rest. Before 1.0, a `feat` or a `!` bumps
the minor version and a `fix` the patch version. To ship:

1. To reword an entry, edit `CHANGELOG.md` on the release pull request's branch, as the last step
   before you merge: the next merge to `main` closes the release pull request and opens a new one
   without the edit. The first release after you add a crate comes out as a patch when only that
   crate changed. For a minor one, change the version on that branch too: in the root
   `Cargo.toml`, in `Cargo.lock` and in the `CHANGELOG.md` heading. Its notes can repeat older
   entries; delete those. Renaming the main crate stops the release workflow with an error that
   says what to do by hand.
2. Under each breaking change, from a commit marked `!` or with a `BREAKING CHANGE:` footer, write
   what users must do before they upgrade. Edit `CHANGELOG.md` on the release pull request's
   branch, as in step 1. [Stability](stability.md) promises users this note.
3. Merge the release pull request once its checks pass. Merge no other pull request until the
   release workflow's **{{ 'Release' if kind == 'library' else 'Tag' }}** job finishes, or
   release-plz may propose the same version again.
{%- if kind != 'library' %}
4. If a later job fails, re-run the failed jobs. They pick up from the same tag.

### Keep releases verifiable

Changing a name that [Stability](stability.md#names-that-stay-the-same) lists breaks users'
scripts, links and the commands they copied from Install and Verify a release.
{%- endif %}
{%- if published %}

## Add a crate to a published library

Every crate you add is published too, and crates.io takes a new crate only by hand. Publish its
first version with `cargo publish --package <crate>`, then add this repository's `release.yml` as
a trusted publisher in its settings on crates.io. Later releases publish it through `release.yml`.
{%- endif %}

## Change an answer

To change an answer, keep the trellis version and pass the new value, such as
{%- if kind == 'service' %} the license:

```sh
uvx copier update --defaults --vcs-ref=:current: --data 'license=MIT OR Apache-2.0'
```
{%- else %} the platforms:

```sh
uvx copier update --defaults --vcs-ref=:current: --data 'platforms=[linux, macos]'
```
{%- endif %}

The change is a rule change, so it can't also change code. Once it merges, run
`just check-github` on `main`, and `just setup-github` if it reports a wrong setting.
{%- if kind != 'service' %}
Changing the platforms needs nothing on GitHub, since `CI passed` covers whatever platforms CI
runs.
{%- endif %}
Some answers also change what GitHub needs:
{% if visibility == 'private' %}
- **Visibility.** To make the repository public, run
  `uvx copier update --defaults --vcs-ref=:current: --data visibility=public` on a branch.
{%- if kind == 'binary' %}
  It adds the release attestations, which fail in a private repository.
{%- elif kind == 'service' %}
  It adds the image attestation and signature, which a private repository can't use.
{%- endif %}
  Make the repository public on GitHub, then run `just setup-github` on the branch. It adds the
  Actions policy, without which a public repository doesn't run the rule-change check, and turns
  on private vulnerability reporting. Only then open the pull request, so the check runs, and
  merge it before any release pull request.
{%- if kind == 'service' %}
  The image's package stays private until you make it public in its package settings, as the
  updated page's "At the first release" says.
{%- endif %}
{%- elif published %}
- **Visibility.** The repository stays public while it publishes to crates.io, and the template
  refuses `visibility=private` until `publish_crate` is off.
{%- else %}
- **Visibility.** To make the repository private, first merge the change that
  `uvx copier update --defaults --vcs-ref=:current: --data visibility=private` makes, then make
  the repository private on GitHub and run `just check-github` on `main`.
{%- if kind == 'service' %}
  The image's package stays public, because GitHub can't make a public package private again.
  Delete it in its package settings before the next release, which then publishes a new, private
  package.
{%- endif %}
  GitHub takes private vulnerability reports only in public repositories, so `SECURITY.md` then
  sends reports to issues, and the Actions policy for the rule-change check no longer matters.
  Protecting `main` in a private repository needs GitHub Pro or a paid organization plan, and its
  Actions minutes come out of the account's included usage.
{%- if kind != 'service' %}
  A macOS minute costs about ten times a Linux one, and a Windows minute about twice.
{%- endif %}
{%- endif %}
{%- if kind == 'library' and visibility == 'public' and not publish_crate %}
- **Publishing.** After turning on `publish_crate`, publish the first version as the updated
  page's "At the first release" says.
{%- if license == 'none' %} crates.io needs a license, so pass one in the same update,
  such as `--data license=MIT`.
{%- endif %}
{%- endif %}

## Update trellis

Renovate opens a pull request when trellis tags a new version. To update by hand:

```sh
uvx copier update --defaults
```

An update never touches `crates/`, `Cargo.lock`, `CHANGELOG.md` or `docs/design.md`, which are
yours after the project is generated.
{%- if published %} The crate's `LICENSE*` files are the exception: they
follow the license answer.
{%- endif %} It is a rule change, so it can't also change code. Copier
merges the new template into your edits; resolve any conflict markers it leaves before you commit.
Once the update merges, run `just check-github` on `main`. A newer trellis can set more than the one
you set up with, so if it reports a wrong setting, run `just setup-github`.

## Renew the release token

A fine-grained token expires on the date you set when you created it. Before it does, create a new
one with the same access and replace the secret:

```sh
gh secret set RELEASE_PLZ_TOKEN
```

Until you do, the release workflow's **Update the release PR** job fails, and the release pull
request stops updating.
